TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Images 1–5; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A 600 km circular sun-synchronous orbit requires a retrograde inclination near 97.8°; the stated 87.7924° is not sun-synchronous at this altitude."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Guiana Space Centre is near 5° N latitude, not 45.05° N. Its latitude is lower than the target inclination, but the report's stated value is erroneous."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The explanation that the orbit passes over almost the entire Earth is overstated: a near-polar orbit does not provide coverage to all latitudes, and the stated inclination also excludes latitudes beyond about 87.8°."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The COE set is incomplete as an epoch and a defined position/phase are needed to specify the orbit. Argument of latitude is a valid alternative for a circular orbit, but the stated RAAN rationale does not establish that its value is the launch-site location."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table appears to cover only a short, selected interval; the reported mean contact time should not be interpreted as an average daily contact duration without specifying the analysis interval and access assumptions."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A passive drag sail is proposed, but no sail sizing, expected deorbit time, or demonstration that the disposal approach meets applicable debris-mitigation guidelines is provided."}],"overall":"A coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept is presented with useful STK access outputs and a plausible single-satellite rationale. Important gaps remain in user identification, orbital accuracy, completeness of design evidence and EOL substantiation. The SSO inclination error is a major technical flaw; presentation and referencing also need improvement.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The mission purpose and need are explained with relevant context, and the single-spacecraft choice is justified. However, the operator and customer groups are not identified explicitly; saying the satellite is operated and accessed from Australian ground facilities does not name its operator or customers. Referencing is present but uneven.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report covers launch, orbit, manoeuvres, ground access, duration and disposal, and includes STK-derived figures and access data. But the SSO inclination is technically incorrect: at 600 km, a sun-synchronous orbit requires roughly 97.8° retrograde, not 87.79°. Launch-site latitude is also misstated, orbital elements are incomplete, and the disposal plan lacks quantified compliance or decay time. Software files are not evidenced.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 87.7924^{\\circ} $"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarizes the selected launch, orbit and ground station and acknowledges launch-vehicle uncertainty. It is relevant but mostly restates design choices and does not draw detailed conclusions from the evidence or address key uncertainties such as imaging performance and the incorrect SSO inclination.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"After analysis of each mission segment, a fully described satellite configuration has been constructed."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable structure, contents, executive summary, figures and bibliography. However, the writing and formatting are inconsistent, figures lack formal numbering/titles in the text, and the bibliography is incomplete and inconsistently formatted. It relies on Wikipedia despite the task explicitly disallowing it. These weaknesses prevent professional-level presentation.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Launch","quote":"To compare existing spaceflight mission plans, a wiki sorted directory – ‘Timeline of spaceflight’ – will be referred to throughout the report [6]."},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
